# Add accepted vulnerability IDs here, one per line.
# Example:
# CVE-2023-44487

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DS-0026 — "Add HEALTHCHECK instruction in your Dockerfile" (LOW, misconfig)
#
# Accepted for both backend/Dockerfile and frontend/Dockerfile.
#
# Why: a Docker HEALTHCHECK is dead weight in these images. The kubelet never
# reads it — health checking for these pods is owned by the liveness and
# readiness probes defined in the charts (collectiongeek-gitops,
# charts/backend and charts/frontend). Worse, the backend runtime stage is
# gcr.io/distroless/static-debian12:nonroot, which ships no shell and no
# curl/wget, so there is literally nothing for a HEALTHCHECK to exec short of
# building a health subcommand into the Go binary purely to satisfy a static
# scanner.
#
# Why here rather than dismissing in the GitHub UI: a UI dismissal is keyed to
# the alert's fingerprint, which includes surrounding file content. Alerts #7
# (backend) and #15 (frontend) were both dismissed "won't fix" on 2026-07-14
# with this same reasoning — then #100 added the Go 1.26.5 pin comment to
# backend/Dockerfile, the fingerprint moved, and the finding came straight back
# as a brand-new alert #46. An ignorefile entry is keyed to the rule ID, so it
# survives edits to the files it covers.
#
# Revisit if either image is ever run outside Kubernetes (plain `docker run`,
# Compose, ECS), where the runtime *does* act on HEALTHCHECK.
DS-0026
